From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: John Ericson <John.Ericson@Obsidian.Systems>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 21:14:10 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] uts: survive a readdir of /dev on a kernel without ZFS

Reading `/dev` on a system without the ZFS module panics the kernel, twice
over, in two adjacent places in `uts/common/fs/dev/sdev_zvolops.c`. Both are
reached the first time any process reads the directory -- a shell searching
`$PATH` is enough.

**1. Freeing a pointer that was never allocated.**
`devzvol_update_zclist_cb()` (:377) is dispatched on a taskq whenever `/dev` is
looked at. It declares `size_t size;` with no initialiser and passes `&size` to
`devzvol_handle_ioctl()`, which returns `ENXIO` *before* it allocates anything
if it cannot open `/dev/zfs`. On that return `size` has never been written, and
`zc` -- from `kmem_zalloc()` -- still has `zc_nvlist_dst == NULL`. The
`default:` arm then frees it unconditionally, i.e.
`kmem_free(NULL, <uninitialised>)`:

    panic[cpu0]: BAD TRAP: type=e (#pf Page fault) addr=c
        occurred in module "genunix" due to a NULL pointer dereference
      genunix:kmem_free+5d
      dev:devzvol_update_zclist_cb+117
      genunix:taskq_thread+2fa

The `case 0:` arm already guards its equivalent free with
`if (devzvol_zclist)`. This makes the error path match the success path, and
initialises `size` so the freed length is well defined on every return.

**2. An assertion that can only ever fire.**
With no pool list to unpack, `devzvol_create_pool_dirs()` (:453) takes the
`if (rc)` arm after `nvlist_unpack()` and immediately does `ASSERT(rc == 0)`
(:469) -- inside a branch entered only when `rc != 0`:

    panic[cpu0]: assertion failed: rc == 0,
        file: ../../common/fs/dev/sdev_zvolops.c, line: 469
      dev:devzvol_create_pool_dirs+1b0
      dev:devzvol_readdir+cb
      genunix:fop_readdir+61
      genunix:getdents64+ff

The recovery code beneath it is correct and does the right thing on a
non-DEBUG kernel; only the assertion is wrong. Drop it, and guard its
`kmem_free()` for the same reason as above -- `devzvol_zclist` is NULL in
exactly this case.

Neither is otherwise noticeable because ZFS is present on essentially every
illumos system, so `devzvol_handle_ioctl()` almost never fails this way and
both arms are close to unreachable in practice. It takes a deliberately
minimal system -- one that wants `/dev` but not ZFS -- to reach them.

Assisted-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 usr/src/uts/common/fs/dev/sdev_zvolops.c | 28 +++++++++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 22 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)

diff --git a/usr/src/uts/common/fs/dev/sdev_zvolops.c b/usr/src/uts/common/fs/dev/sdev_zvolops.c
index 8f22ef32f0..4be960af13 100644
--- a/usr/src/uts/common/fs/dev/sdev_zvolops.c
+++ b/usr/src/uts/common/fs/dev/sdev_zvolops.c
@@ -378,7 +378,7 @@ devzvol_update_zclist_cb(void *arg)
 {
 	zfs_cmd_t	*zc;
 	int		rc;
-	size_t		size;
+	size_t		size = 0;
 
 	zc = kmem_zalloc(sizeof (zfs_cmd_t), KM_SLEEP);
 	mutex_enter(&devzvol_mtx);
@@ -404,9 +404,17 @@ devzvol_update_zclist_cb(void *arg)
 			 * catastrophic error.
 			 *
 			 * Give up memory and exit.
+			 *
+			 * devzvol_handle_ioctl() can fail before it has
+			 * allocated anything -- it returns ENXIO if /dev/zfs
+			 * cannot be opened at all -- in which case zc came
+			 * from kmem_zalloc() and zc_nvlist_dst is still NULL.
+			 * Guard the free the same way the success path above
+			 * guards its own.
 			 */
-			kmem_free((void *)(uintptr_t)zc->zc_nvlist_dst,
-			    size);
+			if (zc->zc_nvlist_dst != 0)
+				kmem_free((void *)(uintptr_t)zc->zc_nvlist_dst,
+				    size);
 			break;
 	}
 
@@ -458,9 +466,17 @@ devzvol_create_pool_dirs(struct vnode *dvp)
 	rc = nvlist_unpack((char *)(uintptr_t)devzvol_zclist,
 	    devzvol_zclist_size, &nv, 0);
 	if (rc) {
-		ASSERT(rc == 0);
-		kmem_free((void *)(uintptr_t)devzvol_zclist,
-		    devzvol_zclist_size);
+		/*
+		 * No assertion here: this arm is reached whenever there is no
+		 * pool list to unpack, which is the normal state of affairs on
+		 * a system with no ZFS at all -- devzvol_update_zclist() will
+		 * have left devzvol_zclist NULL because the ioctl failed. The
+		 * recovery below is correct; asserting rc == 0 inside `if (rc)'
+		 * could only ever fire.
+		 */
+		if (devzvol_zclist != 0)
+			kmem_free((void *)(uintptr_t)devzvol_zclist,
+			    devzvol_zclist_size);
 		devzvol_gen = 0;
 		devzvol_zclist = 0;
 		devzvol_zclist_size = 0;
